1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,356
The app that helped Starbucks collect over 1.7

2
00:00:02,356 --> 00:00:05,240
billion dollars in customer balances also drove customers away.

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:07,896
From July to September 2024,

4
00:00:07,896 --> 00:00:09,951
the number of purchases at U.S.

5
00:00:09,951 --> 00:00:13,000
Starbucks stores fell 10% from a year earlier.

6
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:15,870
Nine customers for every ten who used to come.

7
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:19,620
If you own Starbucks stock or are thinking about it,

8
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:22,060
the next nine minutes lay out how Starbucks

9
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:24,840
makes money and what today's price is betting on.

10
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:28,160
Here's what you'll take away.

11
00:00:28,350 --> 00:00:31,001
One: what the 1.7 billion dollars customers

12
00:00:31,001 --> 00:00:32,850
left with Starbucks really is.

13
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:35,106
Two: why customers stopped coming,

14
00:00:35,106 --> 00:00:36,490
and how they came back.

15
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:40,460
Three: the three numbers to check on October 29 earnings.

16
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:44,546
Here's the strange part: this app was the best

17
00:00:44,546 --> 00:00:46,760
money-maker Starbucks ever had.

18
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:51,050
First, how much money did this app collect?

19
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:54,430
An American commuter orders ahead in the app

20
00:00:54,430 --> 00:00:56,540
and just grabs the cup at the store.

21
00:00:56,750 --> 00:00:58,124
When the balance runs low,

22
00:00:58,124 --> 00:01:00,290
the app reloads 25 dollars automatically.

23
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:03,962
35.8 million active Starbucks Rewards members in the U.S.

24
00:01:03,962 --> 00:01:05,020
use the app this way.

25
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:08,005
They account for 59% of spending at U.S.

26
00:01:08,005 --> 00:01:09,676
company-operated stores.

27
00:01:09,676 --> 00:01:12,670
Six of every ten dollars come from members.

28
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:14,621
In one quarter,

29
00:01:14,621 --> 00:01:18,840
3.1 billion dollars were loaded onto cards and the app.

30
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:22,780
The money reaches Starbucks before the coffee is even made.

31
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:27,527
Money loaded but not yet spent comes to about

32
00:01:27,527 --> 00:01:30,334
1.75 billion dollars in the U.S.

33
00:01:30,334 --> 00:01:30,860
alone.

34
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:32,947
Right after the holiday gift season,

35
00:01:32,947 --> 00:01:34,310
it tops 2 billion dollars.

36
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:36,199
In accounting terms,

37
00:01:36,199 --> 00:01:38,947
this balance is a debt Starbucks repays in coffee.

38
00:01:38,947 --> 00:01:41,860
It takes the money first and serves the coffee later.

39
00:01:42,070 --> 00:01:46,145
But this debt carries no interest and no due date.

40
00:01:46,145 --> 00:01:48,834
If a bank offered me those terms,

41
00:01:48,834 --> 00:01:50,220
I'd borrow today.

42
00:01:51,020 --> 00:01:53,936
So does Starbucks earn big interest on this money?

43
00:01:53,936 --> 00:01:56,210
The answer is smaller than you'd think.

44
00:01:57,010 --> 00:01:58,033
In fiscal 2025,

45
00:01:58,033 --> 00:02:00,489
the twelve months to September 2025,

46
00:02:00,489 --> 00:02:04,310
Starbucks kept 2.94 billion dollars from selling coffee.

47
00:02:04,510 --> 00:02:06,379
Interest from all of its cash,

48
00:02:06,379 --> 00:02:09,308
plus other income, came to 110 million dollars,

49
00:02:09,308 --> 00:02:11,240
about 4% of what coffee earned.

50
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:13,383
Customers can spend the balance any time,

51
00:02:13,383 --> 00:02:15,990
so Starbucks can't lock it up in long-term investments.

52
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:18,412
The books show one more line,

53
00:02:18,412 --> 00:02:20,156
bigger than interest: breakage,

54
00:02:20,156 --> 00:02:23,138
the balances Starbucks estimates will never be spent,

55
00:02:23,138 --> 00:02:24,151
booked as revenue.

56
00:02:24,151 --> 00:02:26,120
222 million dollars in fiscal 2025.

57
00:02:26,330 --> 00:02:29,687
Forgotten gift cards earned twice as much as interest.

58
00:02:29,687 --> 00:02:32,050
Honestly, I have one in my drawer too.

59
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:32,688
Still,

60
00:02:32,688 --> 00:02:35,830
breakage is under 8% of what coffee earned.

61
00:02:36,630 --> 00:02:40,145
So the first answer: the balance isn't money that earns interest.

62
00:02:40,145 --> 00:02:42,200
It's money that brings customers back.

63
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:44,203
With 12 dollars left in the app,

64
00:02:44,203 --> 00:02:46,851
it's hard to try another cafe tomorrow morning.

65
00:02:46,851 --> 00:02:48,090
That's called lock-in.

66
00:02:48,290 --> 00:02:51,230
The problem is, the app worked too well.

67
00:02:52,030 --> 00:02:54,098
Picture the pickup counter at a U.S.

68
00:02:54,098 --> 00:02:55,190
Starbucks at 8 a.m.

69
00:02:55,990 --> 00:02:58,612
Cups for app orders line the pickup counter

70
00:02:58,612 --> 00:03:01,600
while customers at the register wait behind them.

71
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:03,120
Today one in three orders at U.S.

72
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:04,880
company-operated stores comes in through the

73
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:06,160
app before the customer arrives.

74
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:10,560
App orders often come with add-ons like extra syrup or cold foam.

75
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:13,930
Each add-on raises what Starbucks earns per cup.

76
00:03:14,130 --> 00:03:17,490
But it also stretches the time a barista needs to make each drink.

77
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:19,183
In April 2024,

78
00:03:19,183 --> 00:03:22,119
the then-CEO admitted Starbucks was struggling

79
00:03:22,119 --> 00:03:24,097
to keep up with morning demand,

80
00:03:24,097 --> 00:03:27,160
and long waits were turning some customers away.

81
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:30,290
Founder Howard Schultz went as far as calling

82
00:03:30,290 --> 00:03:32,960
the app Starbucks' biggest Achilles heel.

83
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:37,730
Transactions fell 6% in April to June 2024,

84
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:40,500
then 10% in July to September.

85
00:03:40,690 --> 00:03:45,770
The CEO who took over in March 2023 was replaced after 17 months.

86
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:50,035
This isn't the first time Starbucks lost customers.

87
00:03:50,035 --> 00:03:52,616
On February 26, 2008, about 7,000 U.S.

88
00:03:52,616 --> 00:03:54,790
Starbucks stores closed at once.

89
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:58,116
Founder Howard Schultz shut them for three and

90
00:03:58,116 --> 00:04:01,650
a half hours to retrain baristas on making espresso.

91
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:03,741
Back then the cause was the coffee.

92
00:04:03,741 --> 00:04:06,510
This time it was stores overloaded with app orders.

93
00:04:07,310 --> 00:04:08,096
So part two,

94
00:04:08,096 --> 00:04:11,700
the cause: stores slowed down by a flood of app orders.

95
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:14,960
How did Starbucks bring customers back?

96
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:17,416
On August 13, 2024,

97
00:04:17,416 --> 00:04:20,990
Starbucks named Brian Niccol its new CEO.

98
00:04:21,790 --> 00:04:24,818
Niccol had spent six years growing the burrito

99
00:04:24,818 --> 00:04:26,530
chain Chipotle as its CEO.

100
00:04:26,730 --> 00:04:29,940
Starbucks shares jumped 24% intraday on the news,

101
00:04:29,940 --> 00:04:33,280
the biggest intraday gain in the company's history.

102
00:04:34,090 --> 00:04:36,348
His work arrangement made headlines too: he

103
00:04:36,348 --> 00:04:38,711
would fly a company jet more than 1,000 miles

104
00:04:38,711 --> 00:04:40,497
between his home in Newport Beach,

105
00:04:40,497 --> 00:04:42,440
California, and Seattle headquarters.

106
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:45,225
I complain about two subway stops.

107
00:04:45,225 --> 00:04:47,050
His commute is a flight.

108
00:04:47,850 --> 00:04:49,532
Instead of adding new drinks,

109
00:04:49,532 --> 00:04:52,200
Niccol announced he would cut 30% of the menu.

110
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:55,435
Customers staying in got ceramic mugs again,

111
00:04:55,435 --> 00:04:59,090
and baristas started handwriting notes on to-go cups.

112
00:04:59,290 --> 00:05:02,660
He also set a goal: every drink in four minutes.

113
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:05,111
Niccol didn't kill the app.

114
00:05:05,111 --> 00:05:07,803
He focused on making stores fast again after

115
00:05:07,803 --> 00:05:09,760
app orders had slowed them down.

116
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:11,531
It wasn't free.

117
00:05:11,531 --> 00:05:15,478
Spending on staff and stores first meant that in fiscal 2025,

118
00:05:15,478 --> 00:05:19,490
Starbucks kept only 7.9 dollars of every 100 dollars of sales.

119
00:05:19,690 --> 00:05:19,995
U.S.

120
00:05:19,995 --> 00:05:24,730
stores open more than a year also saw sales fall 2% that year.

121
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:28,188
Then, from April to June this year, U.S.

122
00:05:28,188 --> 00:05:30,390
comparable store sales rose 7.9%.

123
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:35,590
What matters is why: the number of purchases rose 4.2%.

124
00:05:36,390 --> 00:05:39,698
Active Rewards members grew from 33.8 million

125
00:05:39,698 --> 00:05:41,830
to 35.8 million in two years.

126
00:05:42,030 --> 00:05:45,610
Not just higher prices: more people are coming back to the stores.

127
00:05:46,410 --> 00:05:51,064
That's the answer to part two: once the stores got fast again,

128
00:05:51,064 --> 00:05:52,490
customers returned.

129
00:05:53,290 --> 00:05:56,300
So how did it go for Starbucks shareholders?

130
00:05:57,110 --> 00:06:00,460
If you had bought 100 million won of Starbucks

131
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:02,281
near its peak on July 23,

132
00:06:02,281 --> 00:06:02,645
2021,

133
00:06:02,645 --> 00:06:05,850
it would be worth 8천만 원 at the Oct 23 close.

134
00:06:06,050 --> 00:06:08,700
Bought the day before the Niccol news,

135
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:10,024
on August 12, 2024,

136
00:06:10,024 --> 00:06:13,720
the same 100 million won would be worth 1억 3천만 원 now.

137
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:14,602
Same company,

138
00:06:14,602 --> 00:06:17,330
very different results depending on when you bought.

139
00:06:18,130 --> 00:06:21,450
So is today's price of $100.8 expensive or cheap?

140
00:06:22,250 --> 00:06:25,072
Let's borrow the method of Warren Buffett's teacher,

141
00:06:25,072 --> 00:06:25,940
Benjamin Graham.

142
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:28,346
Take average earnings over several years and

143
00:06:28,346 --> 00:06:29,650
pay at most 10 times that.

144
00:06:29,850 --> 00:06:32,351
Expected future growth is left out,

145
00:06:32,351 --> 00:06:34,780
so it's a very conservative value.

146
00:06:35,580 --> 00:06:39,208
By this math one Starbucks share is worth $26.16.

147
00:06:39,208 --> 00:06:42,170
At the Oct 23 close it traded at $100.8.

148
00:06:42,370 --> 00:06:44,805
The market pays 3.9x the floor value.

149
00:06:44,805 --> 00:06:48,029
In our Tesla episode the same math gave 38 times,

150
00:06:48,029 --> 00:06:50,530
so Starbucks looks far less expensive.

151
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:53,908
So what is the ValueNote score of a stock that

152
00:06:53,908 --> 00:06:55,090
looks less expensive?

153
00:06:56,440 --> 00:06:59,980
Starbucks scores 21 out of 100 on ValueNote.

154
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:00,997
Broken down,

155
00:07:00,997 --> 00:07:04,741
its financial strength score is 53 and its price score,

156
00:07:04,741 --> 00:07:07,940
how cheap the stock is against the value, is 0.

157
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:12,366
The price score is low because the stock trades at 3.9x the value.

158
00:07:12,366 --> 00:07:16,015
Earnings per share fell by more than half in fiscal 2025,

159
00:07:16,015 --> 00:07:18,320
pulling the multi-year average down.

160
00:07:19,130 --> 00:07:21,982
The company expects to earn 2.55 to 2.65

161
00:07:21,982 --> 00:07:23,978
dollars per share this year,

162
00:07:23,978 --> 00:07:25,760
excluding one-time costs.

163
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:29,175
That's more than 20% above last year's 2.13.

164
00:07:29,175 --> 00:07:32,610
This report is the first check on that outlook.

165
00:07:33,410 --> 00:07:34,994
If the recovery continues,

166
00:07:34,994 --> 00:07:37,066
more customers become more profit,

167
00:07:37,066 --> 00:07:38,834
the multi-year average rises,

168
00:07:38,834 --> 00:07:40,540
and so does the floor value.

169
00:07:40,740 --> 00:07:41,630
If it stalls,

170
00:07:41,630 --> 00:07:44,709
the weak years keep dragging the average down

171
00:07:44,709 --> 00:07:47,310
and the gap with today's price widens.

172
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:51,273
ValueNote runs this calculation daily on more than 600 companies,

173
00:07:51,273 --> 00:07:52,200
Starbucks included.

174
00:07:52,410 --> 00:07:55,622
It's a conservative value that leaves out growth,

175
00:07:55,622 --> 00:07:58,440
not a target price or a buy or sell signal.

176
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:00,632
Finally, part three.

177
00:08:00,632 --> 00:08:02,858
Starbucks reports after the U.S.

178
00:08:02,858 --> 00:08:04,250
close on October 29.

179
00:08:05,050 --> 00:08:07,310
First, U.S. comparable store sales.

180
00:08:07,310 --> 00:08:10,410
The company guided to 6.5% or more this quarter.

181
00:08:10,610 --> 00:08:10,947
Second,

182
00:08:10,947 --> 00:08:13,455
transactions: whether the number of customer visits,

183
00:08:13,455 --> 00:08:14,660
not prices, keeps rising.

184
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:15,339
Third,

185
00:08:15,339 --> 00:08:20,527
how many dollars Starbucks keeps from every 100 dollars of sales.

186
00:08:20,527 --> 00:08:23,480
It expects more than 11 for the year.

187
00:08:24,280 --> 00:08:27,591
In 2008 Schultz closed the stores to retrain on espresso.

188
00:08:27,591 --> 00:08:29,508
In 2024 Niccol brought back mugs,

189
00:08:29,508 --> 00:08:31,890
handwritten notes and a four-minute goal.

190
00:08:32,090 --> 00:08:35,332
Both fixed the in-store experience first.

191
00:08:35,332 --> 00:08:38,179
Whether customer visits keep rising,

192
00:08:38,179 --> 00:08:39,840
October 29 will tell.

193
00:08:40,050 --> 00:08:43,053
And one tip for your own money: load only what

194
00:08:43,053 --> 00:08:44,619
you'll spend in a month.

195
00:08:44,619 --> 00:08:47,100
The interest goes to Starbucks anyway.

